Background
Stiff person syndrome (SPS), first described by Moersch 
and Woltman in 1956, is a rare, highly disabling, progres-
sive autoimmune disorder of the central nervous system 
(CNS) characterized by muscle rigidity and spasms [ 1]. 
Since its original description, several variants of the syn -
drome including stiff limb syndrome, jerking SPS, para -
neoplastic SPS and progressive encephalomyelitis with 
rigidity and myoclonus have been described [ 2]. High 
titres of autoantibodies to glutamic acid decarboxylase 
(GAD), the rate-limiting enzyme in the synthesis of the 
inhibitory neurotransmitter gamma-amino butyric acid 
(GABA), have been reported in approximately 60–80  % 
of patients with classic SPS [ 3]. However, SPS remains a 
clinical diagnosis facilitated by a high index of suspicion. 
SPS has an estimated prevalence of 1/million population 
[4], but is believed to be underdiagnosed [ 5]. Thus far, 
only 14 cases have been reported from South Asia, which 
has a collective population of over 1.7 billion. We report 
the first authenticated case of classic SPS from Sri Lanka.
